.TH CCOL_ITER_VAL_PTR 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_iter_val_ptr \- get a typed pointer to the current value
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/citerators.h>   /* or any container header */

ValT *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it);            /* ValT const * for a char pointer */
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro gives a typed pointer to the value of the element that it points
at now. A change through this pointer also changes the container below it.

For a value type that is a character pointer, the target carries a const
qualifier. The caller therefore reads the stored string, and edits its
bytes in place inside the stored length. The caller does not replace the
string. A map can name only one such object: its own accessor slot. The
companion size field of that slot describes the string that the slot points
at now. A stored pointer that the map did not allocate and did not size
leaves that size for a different string. The next read of the element then
runs past the end of the new string. Use the insert operation of the owning
container to store a different string. Every other value type names the
element itself, and the caller can write through it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
it   Iterator variable that ccol_iter_declare() declares, or an iterator
     inside ccol_for_each().
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ValT* to the current element. The macro gives ValT const* when ValT is a
character pointer.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The pointer stays valid only while the iterator is alive and the
container stays unchanged.
This macro uses a GNU C statement expression. It needs GCC or Clang.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_iter_key_ptr (3),
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3)
